The demonstrated problem is now specific:

The current effective Case-1 rule requires independent historical proof of BOTH:

1. non-consumption of the exact historical Codex bookkeeping ref; and
2. exclusion of substantive aliasing over the relevant historical interval.

Bounded recovery has established that the retained historical system does not provide a complete execution audit, continuous ref-relationship history, transaction ledger, or other primary evidence capable of proving either proposition.

Do not search again for those facts in this lane.
Do not infer either proposition.
Do not convert missing evidence into evidence of absence.

The sole task is to determine whether the EFFECTIVE-ACCEPTED Case-1 requirement is itself too strict for the narrowly defined historical bookkeeping class and therefore creates another structurally impossible continuation condition.

Evaluate the requirement against these design objectives:

- substantive repository authority must remain fail-closed;
- unknown or substantive refs must remain blocking;
- missing historical metadata must remain explicitly unknown;
- no producer, transaction, directness, symbolic target, non-consumption or alias history may be invented;
- the review system must not require historical evidence that was never retained merely to prove that Codex review bookkeeping was harmless;
- the process must not become a blanket “absence of evidence means safe” rule;
- the process must not recreate an archive-everything or complete-system-audit requirement that ordinary review cannot satisfy;
- the process must terminate self-generated Codex bookkeeping loops without allowing real repository drift to escape review.

Determine the narrowest safe alternative decision rule.

Specifically assess whether a historical post-B0 Codex bookkeeping ref can be treated as non-blocking for the bounded C2 governance review when all independently verifiable substantive authority remains unchanged, even though complete historical non-consumption and alias exclusion cannot be proven.

Any proposed alternative must require positive evidence for the substantive boundary, including at minimum:

- exact candidate/package bytes unchanged;
- inherited repository path/content/mode/classification boundary unchanged;
- index and staged state unchanged;
- branch/upstream/origin unchanged;
- local/tracking/fresh live-remote HEAD authority unchanged;
- accepted governance/artifact identities unchanged;
- no observed substantive-ref additions, removals or value updates;
- no observed eligible-ref OID updates or remove/re-add laundering;
- all retained ref observations/deltas preserved exactly;
- no affirmative evidence that the historical Codex ref was used as substantive repository/governance authority;
- no affirmative evidence of substantive aliasing;
- no contradiction in available historical evidence.

Carefully distinguish:

- `no affirmative evidence of substantive consumption or aliasing after bounded relevant review`; from
- `positive proof that consumption/aliasing never occurred`.

The first may be considered as part of a narrowly bounded historical governance rule.
The second must not be falsely claimed.

Assess whether the claim ceiling can safely be limited to:

“Available authenticated evidence does not establish substantive drift or substantive use of the historical Codex bookkeeping ref; unresolved producer, transaction, direct/symbolic, target, complete non-consumption and complete alias history remain U. For this exact governance-review boundary only, those unresolved bookkeeping facts do not independently block review when every substantive authority invariant independently passes.”

Do not adopt that wording automatically. Review whether it is actually safe under stronger governance.

Explicitly evaluate abuse cases including:

- a real branch/tag/governance-ref change hidden behind endpoint equality;
- a Codex ref affirmatively used by `git log --all` or another substantive decision;
- an unknown namespace;
- a B0-present ref deletion;
- same-name OID mutation;
- remove/re-add with changed OID;
- available evidence indicating symbolic aliasing to substantive authority;
- incomplete substantive repository boundary;
- missing remote authentication;
- candidate or index drift;
- deliberate evidence destruction;
- future avoidable retention failures;
- attempts to reuse the rule for unrelated repositories, candidates or operational work;
- attempts to convert the bounded result into historical provenance PASS or exact continuity proof.

Also assess whether future systems should retain enough information prospectively to avoid needing this historical rule again, while avoiding blanket archival of all transient observations.

This is governance reassessment only.

Do not apply any new rule to the current C2 boundary in this lane.
Do not modify existing accepted B1 or correction files.
Do not stage, commit, push or publish.
Do not re-run the C2 substantive integration review.
Do not adjudicate the historical 3,288-byte incident.
Do not perform CD reasoning, implementation, qualification, Execution Readiness closure or mining-readiness inference.

